ENCS 5412 switch — HYPERVISOR bundle
====================================

This directory ships inside the bootstrap VM but is NOT meant to run here.
Copy it to the Proxmox host and install it there.

WHY
---
The bootstrap VM's only job is to push firmware into the Marvell ASIC over
PCIe. It carries no traffic and cannot reach the switch's management IP.

The switch answers on 169.254.1.0 over VLAN 2363 on the X710 backplane NIC —
and that NIC belongs to the hypervisor. So management lives on the host.

    front gi0-gi7 -> Marvell ASIC -> te1/te2 10G -> host X710 -> you
                        ^ PCIe
                        |
                  bootstrap VM (this machine — boot only)

INSTALL
-------
From the Proxmox host, with <vm-ip> being this VM:

    scp -r root@<vm-ip>:/opt/encs-host /root/
    bash /root/encs-host/install.sh

install.sh will:
  * find the X710 backplane port (it does NOT hardcode a name — it is
    enp8s0f1np1 on one box and something else on the next)
  * create VLAN interface sw2363 with 169.254.1.1/16, MTU 9216
    (the VLAN iface CANNOT be called enp8s0f1np1.2363 — 16 chars exceeds the
    15-char IFNAMSIZ limit and ip link rejects it)
  * persist that to /etc/network/interfaces
  * install encs-switch-tui, encs-switch-api and encs-switch-vnet to
    /usr/local/sbin
  * install and enable encs-switch-replay.service
  * verify the switch answers
  * OFFER to create swbr0 (see below) - it explains what changes and does
    nothing unless you answer yes. Skipped when stdin is not a terminal, or
    with ENCS_NO_VNET=1.

Then just run:

    encs-switch-tui          press ? for the built-in manual

Seven views have a tab and a hotkey: Ports, VLANs, PoE, MAC, Stats, Config,
Help. TAB opens a menu with the rest — spanning tree, storm control, port
mirroring, static MACs, LLDP/CDP, LACP tuning, private VLANs, ACLs, QoS,
802.1X, RADIUS, IGMP snooping and L3.

Inside a menu view the keys are always the same: ENTER edits the selected
row, SPACE toggles its main setting, 'g' reaches that view's global settings,
'n'/'d' create and delete, ESC cancels. Nothing is written until the last
prompt is answered.

NOTE: the switch boots with SPANNING TREE OFF. Nothing breaks a cabling loop
until you turn it on (TAB -> stp -> g -> enable).

UPDATING
--------
These host tools are independent of the disk image — the ISO and qcow2 only
carry the bootstrap VM. Updating them NEVER requires rebuilding anything.

    encs-switch-tui --version         what is installed
    encs-switch-tui --check-update    is there anything newer?
    encs-switch-tui --update          fetch and install it (needs root)

The TUI also checks once at startup and says so in the title bar. Hosts with
no route to github.com see nothing and are not slowed down;
ENCS_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1 skips the check entirely.

--update verifies SHA256SUMS, refuses anything that will not compile, and
backs up what it replaces into /var/backups/encs-switch/<timestamp>/. It
writes only to /usr/local/sbin, /etc/systemd/system and /opt/encs-host —
never to /etc/encs-switch or your network config.

To update by hand instead, re-copy this directory from a newer checkout and
re-run install.sh; it is idempotent. Or just scp encs-switch-tui over the
installed one — it is a single stdlib-only Python script.

VMs ON THE FRONT PORTS
----------------------
By default a Proxmox VM lands on vmbr0 - the MGMT CPU jack - so guest traffic
shares the interface you manage the host over. NFVIS does not do that: its
lan-br is a bridge on the 10G backplane (int-LAN = te2), and a VM reaches a
GE1/x jack because its frames leave te2 in a VLAN that jack is in. Nothing
attaches a VM to a switch port directly; the front ports are not host NICs.

encs-switch-vnet builds the same thing here - one VLAN-aware bridge on the
backplane NIC, plus the VLAN on the ASIC. install.sh offers to run the first
step for you; these do it later, or again:

    encs-switch-vnet init                  plan the bridge (writes nothing)
    encs-switch-vnet init --yes            write it, then: ifreload -a
    encs-switch-vnet add 100               pick jacks from a list
    encs-switch-vnet add 100 --ports gi0   or name them: VLAN 100 -> GE1/0
    qm set 901 --net1 virtio,bridge=swbr0,tag=100
    encs-switch-vnet status                both halves, and which VM is where
    encs-switch-vnet teardown              undo it - VMs go back to vmbr0

The TUI has the same thing under TAB -> vnet: n creates the bridge, D removes
it, ENTER assigns a front jack to the selected VLAN. Both print the diff of
/etc/network/interfaces and write nothing until you confirm; neither runs
'ifreload -a' for you.

The stanzas live in /etc/network/interfaces itself, between markers. Proxmox
does not read sourced files - its own header says so - so a bridge in
interfaces.d would be invisible to the GUI and unpickable when creating a VM.
Every stanza carries a '#' comment line, which is what the GUI shows in that
interface's Comment column.

Teardown leaves the SWITCH alone - front ports keep their VLANs and keep
replaying from /etc/encs-switch. It only gives this host's backplane NIC back.

init MOVES sw2363 onto the bridge. It has to: once the backplane NIC is a
bridge port, a VLAN subinterface of that NIC receives nothing. Your Proxmox
management interface keeps every setting it has (init only adds a comment line
to its stanza, saying guests belong on swbr0; teardown removes it again), and
neither is the bootstrap VM touched - it
carries no traffic and belongs on vmbr0.

Untagged (no tag= at all) is switch VLAN 1, which every front port is in by
default - the equivalent of NFVIS's stock lan-net.

None of this is privileged. Skipping the tool entirely and building your own
bridge over the backplane NIC works exactly as well - the tool's job is to keep
the host and switch halves consistent and to save the switch side so a cold
boot does not lose it.

Unlike NFVIS, Proxmox cannot hide a bridge: anyone editing a VM's hardware sees
vmbr0 and swbr0 both. A VM on the wrong one just gets the wrong network.

CONTENTS
--------
  install.sh                   host installer (run on Proxmox)
  encs-switch-tui              the management UI; also --save/--apply/--status
  encs-switch-api              low-level XML API client for scripting
  encs-switch-vnet             VM <-> front-port networks (bridge + VLAN)
  encs-switch-replay.service   reapplies config after a cold boot

THE TWO THINGS TO REMEMBER
--------------------------
1. Config is volatile. The ASIC has no flash. VM restarts are fine — they do
   not power-cycle it — but any real power loss wipes the config back to
   VLAN 1 + 2363 with every front port SHUT and PoE off. Save your config
   (TUI: c then w) and let encs-switch-replay put it back. Only the areas
   you have actually configured are saved, so a plain L2 switch writes five
   files rather than thirty.

   If you configure a RADIUS server, /etc/encs-switch/70-radius.xml holds
   its shared secret in clear text — the switch reports it back that way and
   it cannot be replayed without it. That file is created 0600 and the
   directory 0700. Think before committing it anywhere.

2. Never restart the bootstrap service, and avoid stopping the VM. The loader
   can only bootstrap an ASIC in WFI; re-running it against a live switch
   wedges it, and the only recovery is PHYSICAL AC REMOVAL — a CIMC power-off
   is not enough. Note that a wedged loader does not mean a dead switch: check
   with 'ping 169.254.1.0', not with the log.

CREDENTIALS
-----------
  Switch (encs-switch-tui / encs-switch-api):  cisco / cisco

That is the ROS firmware default, not a choice this project made — Cisco's own
switch_settings.py hardcodes it for the un-provisioned case. NFVIS would
replace it and keep the new one in ConfD, which we do not run.

Since the ASIC has no flash, a changed switch password is lost on every cold
boot and reverts to cisco/cisco.

Override per-session:  SW_USER=admin SW_PASS=secret encs-switch-tui

The management VLAN (2363, 169.254.1.0/16) is link-local on the internal
backplane and not routable from your LAN unless you bridge or NAT it
deliberately. Given the credentials reset on power loss, keep it that way.
